WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Hello everyone. Thank you for joining me this month for our monthly workflow.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.000
My name is Sam Edwards, and I'm a Product Manager here at Posit.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.000
This month, I'm excited to talk to you about AI Governance in Workbench.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.000
We're going to look at an AI policy and talk about how we can implement it

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.000
for data scientists using Posit Workbench, Posit Connect, and Posit Package Manager.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Let's start with a common question that we at Posit are hearing from our customers.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.000
They want to know, can we let the data science team use AI?

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.000
You can't say no. AI is so helpful for data scientists to get their work done,

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.000
and even if you did say no, they'd probably find a way to use it anyways,

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.000
whether on their phones, their personal accounts, etc.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.000
On the other hand, you can't say yes.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.000
There's risks using AI. What if they use it in a way that's against your company's policy,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.000
or if data that is not supposed to be exposed is?

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.000
That is, you can't say yes unless you have a way to enforce your organization's AI policy.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.000
Throughout this workflow, that's what we're going to look at today.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.000
On the screen, I have an example AI policy, and we're going to look at how we can

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.000
apply this policy using Workbench, Connect, and Package Manager.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.000
Before we get into the specifics, let's take a moment and step back

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:27.000
and talk about why Posit and why we care so much about responsible AI.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.000
At Posit, we've always cared about something called the Prime Directive.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.000
The Prime Directive comes from John Chambers, who created the S-language at Bell Labs.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:41.000
The S-language eventually became R. In 2008, he wrote down a rule called the Prime Directive.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:47.000
Software for data analysis must be trustworthy, and it must be shown to be trustworthy.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:51.000
We believe that code is the only way to satisfy the Prime Directive.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:55.000
A person can read code, a person can review it, they can test it, and run it again.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:01.000
You can't do that with a point-and-click tool, and you can also not do that with a conversation with a chatbot.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:06.000
We hold this Prime Directive lens to everything we're building with AI as well.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.000
We ask whether it can be trusted, and whether that trust can be shown.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.000
In terms of doing responsible AI, there's four principles that we have developed at Posit.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.000
Keep in mind, this is our current best thinking.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:27.000
The AI environment is changing so quickly, these principles might change as well.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.000
But the first principle is code first.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.000
We believe this is the only way to satisfy the Prime Directive, and nothing changes with the use of AI here.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:41.000
Code is inspectable and reviewable, and is the only way to show the analysis is trustworthy.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.000
Second, we believe that a human should be in the loop.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.000
The human is the expert, and ultimately the accountable person.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:54.000
The AI is the co-pilot, helping the pilot work more efficiently.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Next, we believe that the AI should be session-aware, yet data-private.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.000
And these two things are often in tension with one another.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:09.000
Session-aware means the AI has access to all the context it needs to do correct analysis.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:17.000
However, you don't want to expose sensitive data to an LLM or some model provider where that data is not allowed to go.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.000
And then fourth, governed environments.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.000
A governed environment means the system enforces compliance, not the individual.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.000
At Posit, we often talk about the pit of success.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.000
The pit of success is where someone does the right thing without even trying.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.000
The system is set up in such a way that they have no choice but to fall into the pit of success.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Throughout this workflow, we're going to look at how we can use Posit Workbench, Posit Connect, and Posit Package Manager to force users into the pit of success.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.000
Okay, let's start by talking about what we are actually governing.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.000
There's a few different tools here.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.000
The first is Posit Assistant.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:57.000
Posit Assistant is a coding harness built for data science.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:03.000
You can use it with many different models and many different model providers, and it's built into RStudio and Positron.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:05.000
Next is Posit Workbench.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:12.000
This is where your team writes their code in Positron, RStudio, Jupyter, and VS Code, and where they actually use the Posit Assistant.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.000
Next is Posit Connect.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.000
This is where your data science team deploys their finished work.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.000
It could be interactive apps like a Shiny app.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:23.000
It could be a chatbot using Elmer or Chatless.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.000
And you can also deploy MCP servers to Posit Connect.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.000
Next is Posit Package Manager.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:38.000
This is where your team installs R and Python packages from, Node.js packages, VS Code extensions, and most recently, and relevant to this workshop, is agent plugins.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.000
We'll look at this more later.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.000
And lastly, this diagram depicts how all these tools come together.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.000
We have our AI policy, which is enforced from the top.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:50.000
We use Posit Workbench to enforce that policy.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.000
Posit Connect and Posit Package Manager play a role as well.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.000
And then users are doing their work in Workbench, in Positron, or RStudio, with the Posit Assistant.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.000
Okay, let's start to apply the policy.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:11.000
The first two items on the policy say AI coding assistance is off by default, and only approved groups can turn it on.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:16.000
And then second, contractors and the regulated analytics team get no access to AI.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.000
So two very specific things.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:23.000
To apply these two policies, there is a single configuration file.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:31.000
Workbench has a notion of profiles where you can apply different settings to different users and groups based on who they are.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:36.000
In this example here, we have the Posit Assistant turned off by default.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.000
This applies to everyone based on the star syntax.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:46.000
So if a user logs in, they will not be able to use the Posit Assistant unless they're in the data science group.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:50.000
So the next key, we say assistance enabled equals one.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.000
This means anyone in the data science group can use the Posit Assistant.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:59.000
And then lastly, we are explicitly excluding a known contractor.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.000
We're saying they do not get access.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:07.000
These names and groups come directly from your identity provider, whether that be Okta or Entra.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:12.000
So you're not requiring the Workbench admin to create these groups and keep them up to date.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:15.000
They're coming from your centralized source of truth.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:22.000
So with a few lines of configuration, we now have complete control over who can use the AI features in Workbench.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:28.000
So we've covered the first two items. Let's take a look at the next one.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:35.000
The next two policies say that only approved models can be used and all model traffic must terminate in our own tenancy.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:38.000
Let's look at how we will achieve this.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.000
There's a few different controls that are called out in this model.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:48.000
So the first is we need to say which vendors you can use or which model providers, for example, Bedrock, OpenAI, Anthropic.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:53.000
We then need to specify which models from those vendors are allowed for use.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:57.000
And then lastly, there's some concerns around networking and where the traffic terminates.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:01.000
Let's look at the first item, which is providers.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:09.000
Workbench has a new configuration file called providers.json, where you can control which model providers your users have access to.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:13.000
This is an enforced setting. There's no way for users to override this.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:17.000
So in our case, the only approved model provider is Bedrock.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:23.000
So by default, we turn everything off and then we have a key that says Bedrock and we've enabled it to true.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:28.000
We also have a AWS key and here we apply some specific settings.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:34.000
This is enforced. There's no way now for a user to use a model provider other than Bedrock.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:41.000
To actually apply this file, we're going to use the RStudio profiles again.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:50.000
So under the data science key, you can see we have a new setting, AI providers enforced, and we pointed it at that configuration file that we just created.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:55.000
So now anyone in this group will have those settings enforced.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:01.000
This is a pretty simple example, but you can imagine more complex examples where there's many users, many groups.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:08.000
And if we want to apply different rules, we can do so with many different providers.json files.

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:11.000
Next, we're going to talk about model access.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:18.000
So under the Bedrock key, we've added a new field, models allow, and in here we've listed Sonnet 4.5.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:24.000
In our imaginary company, we're enforcing the AI policy for the only approved model is Sonnet 4.5.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:26.000
So we've listed it here.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:35.000
When a user opens Positron or RStudio and they open the Posit Assistant, the only model in the dropdown they will see is Sonnet 4.5.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:38.000
The only model provider they will see is Bedrock.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:42.000
It's not possible for them to use anything else. It's enforced.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:53.000
I've started a Positron session here in Workbench and I want to show you that I only have access to Bedrock and I only have access to Sonnet 4.5.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:09:01.000
I have the Posit Assistant here open and if I click on settings and then configure LLM providers, the only provider is Bedrock.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:04.000
It's not possible for me to add any other providers.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:10.000
And I also wanted to show the only model I have access to is Sonnet 4.5.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:13.000
Again, it's the only one in the dropdown. It's not possible for me to change.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:16.000
And I can ask the Posit Assistant a question and start working.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:24.000
I can say, hey assistant, please clean the data for me.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:27.000
And it will start working.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:30.000
And lastly, let's talk about networking.

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:34.000
So first we want to clarify what Posit the company sees.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:38.000
Posit the company does not see anything.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:41.000
We don't see any of your AI activity or any of your conversations.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:46.000
Posit Assistant is a harness built into Positron and RStudio.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:50.000
And that harness can see everything that it needs to do effective work.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:54.000
So the Posit Assistant can read your active session.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:57.000
It can see the data frames you have, the variables you have.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:00.000
It can see the columns of those data frames, the types.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.000
And this allows it to do effective and correct data analysis.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:08.000
It can also write code and read code in the console,

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:11.000
which makes it really effective at iterating through.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:15.000
And so to clarify, Posit the company doesn't see anything,

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:19.000
but the Posit Assistant, the harness running on your infrastructure,

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:22.000
has access to everything it needs to do effective analysis.

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:26.000
And there's one more layer on networking as well.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:28.000
We have support for AI gateways.

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:31.000
So if your organization is using an LLM or AI gateway,

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:33.000
you can enforce all traffic through it.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:35.000
There's two places where you configure that.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:39.000
You can see here in Positron settings,

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:43.000
we've configured an endpoint that traffic has to go through.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:47.000
And also in our providers.json, we've modified the base URL

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:50.000
so that it's going through your AI gateway.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:53.000
These are all configurable, and they're also enforced,

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:55.000
so users cannot override it.

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:00.000
Okay, so we've now finished number three and four.

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:04.000
We've configured it so that only Bedrock can be used,

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:07.000
and we've also allowed only Sonnet 4.5,

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:10.000
and we've also set up a LLM gateway

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:12.000
that we're forcing all traffic through.

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:16.000
Next, we'll look at policy number five,

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:20.000
which says no individual can hold a model provider API key.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:23.000
You can imagine in the context of Bedrock,

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:25.000
a user would log into AWS.

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:28.000
They would generate the temporary keys they need,

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:31.000
paste them into their environment, and start working.

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:33.000
This is not a great experience for users

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:35.000
because it involves copying and pasting,

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:37.000
and it's also not secure

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:40.000
because those keys could potentially be leaked.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:42.000
So Workbench has several integrations

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:43.000
for different model providers,

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:46.000
where an admin sets up the integration,

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:49.000
and then when a user logs into Workbench, it just works.

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:51.000
Today, we have this for Bedrock,

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:53.000
Snowflake Cortex, and Microsoft Foundry.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:56.000
And for other model providers not listed here,

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:59.000
users can log in through OAuth or API key,

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:01.000
and we look to provide better support

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:03.000
for those in the future as well.

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:07.000
So in our case, we're using Bedrock.

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:09.000
The admin sets it up.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:10.000
A user logs into Workbench.

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:13.000
They never need to see or touch an API key.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:14.000
It's secure by default.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:18.000
Our next policy is around AI usage.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:20.000
Policy number six says we must report

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:23.000
who used AI, how much, and for any period.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:25.000
So I'm going to break this into two sections.

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:27.000
There's some stuff that we can't answer today,

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:28.000
and then there's some other things

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:30.000
that are more exploratory

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:33.000
that we're working on building into the products.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:37.000
So the first is Workbench auditing and Posit Chronicle.

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:39.000
There's all sorts of activity that gets logged

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:42.000
about how users are using Posit's products.

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:44.000
Again, this logging doesn't go to Posit.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:47.000
It stays on-premise in your environment

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:48.000
and is available to you.

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:50.000
You can see things like what kind of sessions,

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:52.000
are people starting, or how long,

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:54.000
and all this data gets aggregated

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.000
and centralized in Posit Chronicle

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:58.000
where you can query and report on it.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:01.000
Number two is console auditing.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:03.000
In RStudio and in Positron,

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:07.000
there's a console where users can execute R and Python code.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:10.000
It's possible to audit all of the activity in here

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:14.000
by turning on the audit R console setting.

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:15.000
For Positron, right now,

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:17.000
we only have support for monitoring the input

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:20.000
and in RStudio, we can monitor the input and the output.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:24.000
And then lastly, item number three,

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:25.000
within the Posit Assistant,

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:29.000
we give you some token level or session level metrics

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:31.000
about your token usage.

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:33.000
You can see how much you're spending

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:35.000
and we give you insights about the context

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:36.000
and how it's being used.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:39.000
This is helpful for using AI in a responsible way,

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:41.000
making sure you don't have any runaway sessions

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:42.000
that get out of control.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:44.000
There's also lots of more experimental things

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:46.000
that we're thinking about implementing

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:48.000
into our products as well.

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:51.000
The first is centralized utilization metrics.

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:55.000
We're experimenting with ways to collect AI usage

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:57.000
across Workbench, across Connect,

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:00.000
and let admins query from a centralized location.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:01.000
This is still experimental,

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:04.000
but hopefully we'll have more to show here soon.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:06.000
We're also looking at integrating agency-based

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:08.000
and user-based metrics.

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:10.000
Hopefully we'll have more to show here soon.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:12.000
We're also looking at integrating AgentsView

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:13.000
into Workbench.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:16.000
AgentsView lets users look at historical sessions

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:18.000
they've had, identify trends in it,

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:21.000
and understand how they're using AI.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:23.000
It's a very powerful feature

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:27.000
and can be used to improve your AI patterns.

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:30.000
We're also looking at implementing cost controls.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.000
This is really important for model providers

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:36.000
like Bedrock that don't provide granular user level

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:38.000
cost controls out of the box.

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:39.000
And then lastly,

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:42.000
we're looking at agent versus human attribution

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:43.000
in our console logging.

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:46.000
So right now it's possible for the Posit Assistant

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:49.000
to execute our Python code in the console,

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:51.000
which is very important for the Assistant

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:54.000
to be able to effectively loop and do correct analysis.

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:58.000
What we're looking at adding is a record

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:00.000
that indicates whether the code was executed

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.000
by a human or by an agent.

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:03.000
Stay tuned for this.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:05.000
Should be available soon.

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:10.000
Okay, so we looked at number six on reporting.

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:12.000
There's some stuff that's already built

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.000
and some stuff that will be coming soon

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:17.000
and will improve the monitoring story.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:20.000
Okay, now policy number seven

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:23.000
around controlling the packages and agents can use.

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:25.000
So agents can install packages

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:28.000
only from improved internal repositories.

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:29.000
When we say packages,

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:30.000
there's two different angles

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.000
that we're going to take on this.

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:34.000
The first is R and Python packages.

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:36.000
A package manager already does this.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:37.000
With package manager,

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:39.000
you can have curated repositories

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:42.000
where users are only allowed to install

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:45.000
a approved list of R or Python packages.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:49.000
These same settings will apply to agents as well

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:51.000
because it's configured globally

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:53.000
at the Workbench and Connect level.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:55.000
It won't be possible for an agent to install a package

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:58.000
that's not been explicitly approved

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:00.000
in the case of a curated repository.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.000
And then we can also be a little less verbose.

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:04.000
We can have block list rules

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:06.000
so we can block all packages

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:07.000
based on certain characteristics.

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:08.000
For example,

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:10.000
packages that have no vulnerabilities

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:13.000
or packages with a specific license type.

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:14.000
And more recently as well,

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:17.000
we added support for a cooldown age.

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:20.000
So you can specify the packages must be older

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:21.000
than a certain threshold,

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:23.000
for example, 24 hours.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:24.000
And again,

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:25.000
you don't need to do anything separate

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:27.000
for your agent usage here.

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:29.000
Package manager has already been configured

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:32.000
and the agent will use that just like users.

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:36.000
What's more new and novel

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:39.000
is package manager now has support for agent plugins.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:40.000
You can think of agent plugins

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:44.000
as a way to extend the capability of your models.

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:47.000
They can include skills, commands, hooks,

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:49.000
MCP server definitions.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:50.000
So as an admin,

00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:54.000
you can define the plugins that are allowed to be used

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:57.000
and then make them easily available to users.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:01.000
So let's take a look at an example.

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:04.000
In this configuration file that we're looking at,

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:08.000
we have a strict known marketplaces key.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:11.000
And in here we define a single plugin

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:13.000
that users are allowed to use.

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:14.000
And you can see it's pointing

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:17.000
to a Posit package manager instance.

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:20.000
And then we also make this enabled by default

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:22.000
using the extra known marketplaces key.

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:25.000
So when a user starts Positron for the very first time,

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:28.000
this plugin is going to automatically be there.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:33.000
We're now looking at Posit package manager.

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:36.000
You can see I have several different repositories

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:37.000
set up here.

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:40.000
I have CRAN, VS Code extensions with OpenVSX,

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:42.000
agent plugins, and PyPI.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:45.000
Let's focus on agent plugins.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:48.000
If I click on here and explore the packages,

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:50.000
I can see I have one plugin.

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:52.000
It's the simple English plugin.

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:53.000
And if I click on it,

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:56.000
it will give me instructions on how to do configuration

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:59.000
based on the tool I'm using.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:02.000
Now let's jump back to my Positron session.

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:05.000
If I click on settings, plugins,

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:07.000
this opens the plugin view.

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:09.000
And if I go to marketplaces,

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:11.000
you can see the simple English plugin.

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:12.000
It's already here.

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:13.000
There's a lock.

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:14.000
So it's not possible for me

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:16.000
to remove this extension as a user.

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:19.000
And it's also not possible for me to add more extensions.

00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:20.000
It's grayed out here.

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:21.000
I can't click on it

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:24.000
because the admin has to find the ones that are allowed.

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:25.000
And again, this shows up

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:29.000
without the user having to do anything.

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:31.000
So number seven, we've controlled

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:34.000
which R and Python packages agents can install.

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:37.000
And we've also extended the capabilities of them

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:42.000
using agent plugins with Posit Package Manager.

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:46.000
Next, we're going to look at governing AI content.

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:47.000
Policy number eight says

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:49.000
AI-powered content needs a named owner,

00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:53.000
an access list, and a deployment record.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:55.000
So there's a few ways we're going to do this

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:57.000
on Posit Connect.

00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:00.000
First is Posit Connect has support for MCP servers.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.000
You can deploy MCP servers

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:05.000
that extend the capability of your agents.

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:08.000
And then you can enforce the use of those MCP servers

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:11.000
using plugins like we saw in the last section.

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:12.000
And then with Posit Connect,

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:15.000
you can also deploy AI-powered content.

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:18.000
By AI-powered content, I mean a few different things.

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:21.000
You can deploy chatbots to Connect

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:23.000
using packages like Elmer and ChatList.

00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:26.000
And then you can also deploy content

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:28.000
that uses AI in its generation.

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:30.000
For example, a Cordo document

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:35.000
that used RAG as part of its analysis.

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:37.000
We're now looking at Posit Connect.

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:41.000
And what I want to show is a Shiny for Python application

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:43.000
that integrates the chatbot.

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:46.000
And then I'll show how you have control

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:52.000
over who has access to this content.

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:54.000
Posit Connect gives you a few things

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:56.000
that are going to help with this policy.

00:19:56.000 --> 00:19:58.000
So every single piece of content on Connect

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:00.000
has a named owner.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:02.000
So that is a person who is accountable

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:04.000
for that piece of content.

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:06.000
There's an access control list.

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:08.000
So you can control which users

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:10.000
or which groups have access to that content.

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:13.000
And there's also a deployment record.

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:15.000
You can see when content was deployed,

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:16.000
when it was updated,

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:19.000
and when important settings were changed.

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:21.000
This is the chat interface.

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:28.000
We can ask it a question.

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:30.000
And then if we go over to settings here,

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.000
we have complete control

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:35.000
over who has access to this chatbot.

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:39.000
In this case, you can see it's only myself and Abby.

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:41.000
No one else other than the people defined in here

00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:43.000
can use this.

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:45.000
I'm also going to show the audit trail

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:48.000
and how it includes a bunch of information

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:51.000
about how Connect is being used.

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:54.000
If I go back to content, and then system,

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:56.000
and then audit logs,

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:58.000
because I'm an admin on this instance,

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:01.000
I can see information like audit logs.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:03.000
And here you can see a detailed log

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:05.000
of every action that's been taken.

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:07.000
So just before I use that Shiny app,

00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:10.000
I associate it with an OAuth integration.

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:12.000
And you can see a bunch of other records

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:14.000
like appointments, change in ownerships,

00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:15.000
and other things.

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:16.000
So there's a complete audit trail

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:21.000
of everything that's happening on Posit Connect.

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:23.000
So with that, policy number eight is complete.

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:26.000
We're using Posit Connect to have a audit trail

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:29.000
and accountability for all the AI content

00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:33.000
that's running in your environment.

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:34.000
Okay, we've looked at a lot of different

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:36.000
configuration files and settings,

00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:38.000
so I want to take a moment to step back

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:41.000
and review some of what we've done.

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:44.000
So there's three configuration files we mostly used.

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:46.000
The Workbench profiles file,

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:49.000
we looked at enforced provider settings,

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:51.000
and we also looked at some enforced

00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:53.000
Positron settings as well.

00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:55.000
First, we turned on the Posit Assistant

00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:57.000
for the data science team.

00:21:57.000 --> 00:21:59.000
You can see here, we have Assistant enabled one.

00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:02.000
For all other users, it's disabled.

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:06.000
You can see this take effect.

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:08.000
This is a screenshot of Positron

00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:11.000
when the Posit Assistant has been enabled.

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:13.000
You can see you have access to it here,

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:16.000
and this is a screenshot of a user,

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:17.000
in this case, Charles,

00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:19.000
who does not have access to the Posit Assistant.

00:22:19.000 --> 00:22:21.000
It's been disabled,

00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:22.000
and you can see there's no way

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:26.000
for them to access it here.

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:29.000
We also created a providers.json file,

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:30.000
and in this example,

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:32.000
we said only Bedrock is allowed,

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:34.000
and then we've said the only model allowed

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:36.000
is SONNET 4.5.

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:37.000
So in this first screenshot,

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:40.000
you can see Bedrock is the only model provider,

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:41.000
and then in the next screenshot,

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:45.000
we can see SONNET 4.5 is the only model

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:48.000
from Bedrock that users are allowed to use.

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:49.000
And again, with all these settings,

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:51.000
we pushed users into the pit of success.

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:55.000
It's not possible for them to override these settings.

00:22:55.000 --> 00:22:56.000
And then lastly,

00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:59.000
we set up some approved plugins.

00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:01.000
So here, we've said the only plugin

00:23:01.000 --> 00:23:03.000
you're allowed to use is the simple English one

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:05.000
that's hosted on Package Manager,

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:08.000
and we've made it available by default.

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:10.000
And here's a screenshot of Positron,

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:12.000
and it's showing the simple English plugin is there.

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:13.000
It's locked.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:15.000
Users can't remove it,

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:17.000
and the Add button is also grayed out.

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:23.000
It's not possible for users to add additional plugins.

00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:24.000
Okay.

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:25.000
Let's take another step back

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:30.000
and talk about what we've done at a big picture.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:32.000
So we have an AI policy

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:35.000
that is defined in a set of configuration files.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:37.000
You can look at your AI policy

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:39.000
and then look at the configuration

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:41.000
and see how it's applied.

00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:43.000
You have control over the providers,

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:45.000
so we've only allowed Bedrock.

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:46.000
You have control of the models.

00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:48.000
We've only allowed SONNET 4.5,

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:50.000
and you have control over the networking

00:23:50.000 --> 00:23:52.000
through things like using AI gateways,

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:55.000
or you can set up firewall rules as well

00:23:55.000 --> 00:23:59.000
within your infrastructure provider.

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:01.000
Nobody holds an API key.

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:02.000
In the case of Bedrock,

00:24:02.000 --> 00:24:04.000
a user logs into Workbench,

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:06.000
and they have access to Bedrock.

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:08.000
They don't need to refresh API keys,

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:09.000
which is annoying for them,

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:12.000
and there's no risk of them potentially exposing them.

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:13.000
We're using Posit Package Manager

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:15.000
to govern the agent's capabilities,

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:17.000
so we control the R and Python packages

00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:18.000
the agent can use,

00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:21.000
as well as the plugins that it has access to.

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:22.000
And then lastly,

00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:23.000
we're using Posit Connect

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:26.000
as a secure way to deploy MCP servers

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:28.000
and also as a secure way

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:30.000
to deploy AI-powered applications

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:33.000
like chatbots.

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:34.000
Thank you for joining us

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:36.000
on this month's workflow session.

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:37.000
We hope that you are excited

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:38.000
about what we're building,

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:41.000
and we'd be happy to take any questions you have.

